JIT: stale exits live outside their unit

0040 gave every entry point an exit stub (13 instructions, 52 bytes) after its unit's code, for when the block is
erased. Every call's return site is an entry point, so call-dense code carried about 100 bytes of stubs per call
that it never runs, between the code that does: of the 409 bytes x64shape's foot_direct had per call (callee unit
272, the caller's share 137), 104 were stale exits, 182 were code that runs.

The stubs are position independent, and the entry's first instruction reaches them with a B (128 MB either way), so
they need not be in the unit:

- CompileCode emits them behind the unit in the temporary buffer, once the unit's size is known, and copies them to
  this thread's exit area in the same code buffer. ClaimStaleExitSpace takes the areas from the buffer's own
  allocator, 64 KB at a time (or the size of one unit's exits when that is more); a thread starts a new area when
  its area is full or it has moved to another code buffer. If claiming the space moves the thread to a new code
  buffer (the current one was full), the unit is allocated again there: exits and unit stay in one buffer, which is
  what keeps the branch in range and the exits alive as long as the unit.
- SetStaleEntry accepts an exit before the entry as well as after it (a signed 26-bit offset; it required a
  positive one).
- The exits' RIP move is a plain constant load: a relocatable one would record an offset outside the unit. Units
  written to the code cache (off here) are not affected: a block loaded from the cache never had stale exits.
- The area is made coherent with dc cvau / ic ivau directly (SyncStaleExits), not a second ClearICache per unit:
  __builtin___clear_cache is FlushInstructionCache on Windows targets, a system call.
- The areas are not registered as blocks. A host PC in one is in the code buffer but in no block
  (FindBlockHeader returns 0, RestoreRIPFromHostPC falls back to State.rip, which the stub's fourth instruction
  sets); nothing in a stub can fault, and threads are suspended at the suspend checks, not inside stubs.

SQUARK_FEX_STALE_EXIT_INLINE=1 keeps the stubs in the unit, relocatable as before. SQUARK_FEX_STALE_EXIT=0 and =2
mean what they did.

Units get smaller (foot_direct's callee 272 -> 224 bytes with 0045, a 64-call chunk 8,768 -> 5,440) and the stubs'
pages are never executed: with 65,536 functions the code buffer's executable pages go from 25.6 MB to 19.8 MB, and
7.0 MB of stubs stay read-write (vmmap; Wine maps a code page executable when it first runs).

foot_direct, ns per call, the fastest of three alternated rounds (functions: installed runtime -> 0045 -> 0045 and
this patch; this patch without 0045): 1,024: 2.40 -> 2.32 -> 2.30; 2.32. 4,096: 3.11 -> 2.58 -> 2.47; 2.97.
16,384: 4.72 -> 4.74 -> 3.88; 4.10. 65,536: 12.91 -> 12.14 -> 9.41; 10.25. Checksums unchanged. squark
tools/smc-subpage (S9 is the case 0040 was written for), 32,800 calls: 0 stale, 0 with the stubs inline, 2,728 with
SQUARK_FEX_STALE_EXIT=0.

